QuotientGroup__quotientQuotientEquivQuotientAux___proof_1 : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (N : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> (_ : Proof (Subgroup__Normal G N)) -> (M : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> (_ : Proof (Subgroup__Normal G M)) -> (h : Proof (Leq N M)) -> Proof (Leq (Subgroup__map G (HasQuotient__Quotient G (Subgroup G) N) (QuotientGroup__mk' G N) M) (MonoidHom__ker (HasQuotient__Quotient G (Subgroup G) N) (HasQuotient__Quotient G (Subgroup G) M) (QuotientGroup__map G G N M (MonoidHom__id G) h))).

(; //LEAN:QuotientGroup.rangeKerLift._proof_3 ;)
QuotientGroup__rangeKerLift___proof_3 : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (H : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group H)) -> (x2 : Elem (MonoidHom G H)) -> (g : Elem G) -> (hg : Proof (Membership__mem G (Subgroup G) (MonoidHom__ker G H x2) g)) -> Proof (Eqt (Subtype H (x3 => Membership__mem H (Subgroup H) (MonoidHom__range G H x2) x3)) (MonoidHom__rangeRestrict__app x2 g) 1).

(; doc: Given a subgroup `s`, the function that sends a subgroup `t` to the pair consisting of its intersection with `s` and its image in the quotient `α ⧸ s` is strictly monotone, even though it is not injective in general. ;)
(; //LEAN:QuotientGroup.strictMono_comap_prod_image ;)
QuotientGroup__strictMono_comap_prod_image : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group X)) -> (s : Elem (Subgroup X)) -> Proof (StrictMono (Subgroup X) (Prod (Subgroup (Subtype X (x1 => Membership__mem X (Subgroup X) s x1))) (powerset (HasQuotient__Quotient X (Subgroup X) s))) (t => Prod__mk (Subgroup (Subtype X (x2 => Membership__mem X (Subgroup X) s x2))) (powerset (HasQuotient__Quotient X (Subgroup X) s)) (Subgroup__comap (Subtype X (x3 => Membership__mem X (Subgroup X) s x3)) X (Subgroup__subtype X s) t) (Set__image X (HasQuotient__Quotient X (Subgroup X) s) (QuotientGroup__mk X s) (SetLike__coe (Subgroup X) X t)))).

(; //LEAN:RelIso.mk ;)
RelIso__mk : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (r : (Elem X -> Elem X -> Prop)) -> (s : (Elem Y -> Elem Y -> Prop)) -> (toEquiv : Elem (Equiv X Y)) -> (map_rel_iff' : Proof (forall X (a => forall X (b => iff (s (toEquiv a) (toEquiv b)) (r a b))))) -> Elem (RelIso X Y r s).

(; doc: Given the set `s`, `Elem s` is the `Type` of element of `s`.  It is currently an abbreviation so that instance coming from `Subtype` are available. If you're interested in making it a `def`, as it probably should be, you'll then need to create additional instances (and possibly prove lemmas about them). See e.g. `Mathlib/Data/Set/Order.lean`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Elem ;)
Set__Elem : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Set.

(; doc: `Ici a` is the left-closed right-infinite interval $[a, ∞)$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Ici ;)
Set__Ici : (X : Set) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: The preimage of `s : Set β` by `f : α → β`, written `f ⁻¹' s`, is the set of `x : α` such that `f x ∈ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.preimage ;)
Set__preimage : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset Y)) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: The coercion from a term of a `SetLike` to its corresponding `Set`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SetLike.coe ;)
SetLike__coe : (A : Set) -> (B : Set) -> (x : Elem A) -> Elem (powerset B).

(; doc: `x ≈ y` is the distinguished equivalence relation of a setoid. ;)
(; //LEAN:Setoid.r ;)
Setoid__r : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f` is strictly monotone if `a < b` implies `f a < f b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:StrictMono ;)
StrictMono : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A subgroup of a group `G` is a subset containing 1, closed under multiplication and closed under multiplicative inverse.  [Wikidata Q466109](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q466109) ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup ;)
Subgroup : (G : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: A subgroup is characteristic if it is fixed by all automorphisms. Several equivalent conditions are provided by lemmas of the form `Characteristic.iff...` ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.Characteristic ;)
Subgroup__Characteristic : (G : Set) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A subgroup `H` is normal if whenever `n ∈ H`, then `g * n * g⁻¹ ∈ H` for every `g : G`  [Wikidata Q743179](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q743179) ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.Normal ;)
Subgroup__Normal : (G : Set) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> Prop.

(; doc: The center of a group `G` is the set of elements that commute with everything in `G` ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.center ;)
Subgroup__center : (G : Set) -> Elem (Subgroup G).

(; //LEAN:Subgroup.characteristic_iff_comap_eq ;)
Subgroup__characteristic_iff_comap_eq : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> Proof (iff (Subgroup__Characteristic G H) (forall (MulEquiv G G) (x1 => Eqt (Subgroup G) (Subgroup__comap G G (MulEquiv__toMonoidHom G G x1) H) H))).

(; //LEAN:Subgroup.characteristic_iff_map_eq ;)
Subgroup__characteristic_iff_map_eq : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> Proof (iff (Subgroup__Characteristic G H) (forall (MulEquiv G G) (x1 => Eqt (Subgroup G) (Subgroup__map G G (MulEquiv__toMonoidHom G G x1) H) H))).

(; doc: The preimage of a subgroup along a monoid homomorphism is a subgroup. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.comap ;)
Subgroup__comap : (G : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (f : Elem (MonoidHom G N)) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup N)) -> Elem (Subgroup G).

(; doc: The inclusion homomorphism from a subgroup `H` contained in `K` to `K`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.inclusion ;)
Subgroup__inclusion : (G : Set) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> (K : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> (h : Proof (Leq H K)) -> Elem (MonoidHom (Subtype G (x1 => Membership__mem G (Subgroup G) H x1)) (Subtype G (x2 => Membership__mem G (Subgroup G) K x2))).

(; //LEAN:Subgroup.le_normalizer_of_normal ;)
Subgroup__le_normalizer_of_normal : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> (K : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> (_ : Proof (Subgroup__Normal G H)) -> Proof (Leq K (Subgroup__normalizer G (SetLike__coe (Subgroup G) G H))).

(; doc: The image of a subgroup along a monoid homomorphism is a subgroup. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.map ;)
Subgroup__map : (G : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (f : Elem (MonoidHom G N)) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> Elem (Subgroup N).

(; doc: The `normalizer` of `S` is the subgroup of `G` whose elements satisfy `g * S * g⁻¹ = S`. When `S` is a subgroup, this is the largest subgroup of `G` inside which `S` is normal. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.normalizer ;)
Subgroup__normalizer : (G : Set) -> (S : Elem (powerset G)) -> Elem (Subgroup G).

(; doc: Pull a subgroup back to an opposite subgroup along `MulOpposite.unop` ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.op ;)
Subgroup__op : (G : Set) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> Elem (Subgroup (MulOpposite G)).

(; doc: Given `Subgroup`s `H`, `K` of groups `G`, `N` respectively, `H × K` as a subgroup of `G × N`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.prod ;)
Subgroup__prod : (G : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> (K : Elem (Subgroup N)) -> Elem (Subgroup (Prod G N)).

(; doc: If two subgroups `M` and `N` of `G` are equal, their quotients are in bijection. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.quotientEquivOfEq ;)
Subgroup__quotientEquivOfEq : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (Subgroup X)) -> (t : Elem (Subgroup X)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Subgroup X) s t)) -> Elem (Equiv (HasQuotient__Quotient X (Subgroup X) s) (HasQuotient__Quotient X (Subgroup X) t)).

(; doc: For any subgroups `H` and `K`, view `H ⊓ K` as a subgroup of `K`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.subgroupOf ;)
Subgroup__subgroupOf : (G : Set) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> (K : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> Elem (Subgroup (Subtype G (x1 => Membership__mem G (Subgroup G) K x1))).

(; doc: The natural group hom from a subgroup of group `G` to `G`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subgroup.subtype ;)
Subgroup__subtype : (G : Set) -> (H : Elem (Subgroup G)) -> Elem (MonoidHom (Subtype G (x1 => Membership__mem G (Subgroup G) H x1)) G).

(; doc: A _subsingleton_ is a type with at most one element. It is either empty or has a unique element.  All propositions are subsingletons because of proof irrelevance: false propositions are empty, and all proofs of a true proposition are equal to one another. Some non-propositional types are also subsingletons. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subsingleton ;)
Subsingleton : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: If a type is a subsingleton, then all of its elements are equal. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subsingleton.elim ;)
Subsingleton__elim : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Subsingleton X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a b).

(; doc: All the elements of a type that satisfy a predicate.  `Subtype p`, usually written `{ x : α // p x }` or `{ x // p x }`, contains all elements `x : α` for which `p x` is true. Its constructor is a pair of the value and the proof that it satisfies the predicate. In run-time code, `{ x : α // p x }` is represented identically to `α`.  There is a coercion from `{ x : α // p x }` to `α`, so elements of a subtype may be used where the underlying type is expected.  Examples:  * `{ n : Nat // n % 2 = 0 }` is the type of even numbers.  * `{ xs : Array String // xs.size = 5 }` is the type of arrays with five `String`s.  * Given `xs : List α`, `List { x : α // x ∈ xs }` is the type of lists in which all elements are    contained in `xs`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `{ x // p x }` in identifiers is `subtype`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subtype ;)
Subtype : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> Set.

(; //LEAN:Subtype.mk ;)
Subtype__mk : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (val : Elem X) -> (property : Proof (p val)) -> Elem (Subtype X (x => p x)).

(; doc: The value in the underlying type that satisfies the predicate. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subtype.val ;)
Subtype__val : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (self : Elem (Subtype X (x => p x))) -> Elem X.

(; doc: The top (`⊤`, `\top`) element  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⊤` in identifiers is `top`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Top.top ;)
Top__top : (X : Set) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:ZeroHom.mk ;)
ZeroHom__mk : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (toFun : (Elem M -> N)) -> (map_zero' : Proof (Eqt N (toFun 0) 0)) -> Elem (ZeroHom M N).

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; doc: **Alias** of `congrArg`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congr_arg ;)
congr_arg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:inv_mul_cancel ;)
inv_mul_cancel : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times (Inv__inv G a) a) 1).

(; //LEAN:inv_one ;)
inv_one : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (InvOneClass G)) -> Proof (Eqt G (Inv__inv G 1) 1).

(; //LEAN:map_prod ;)
map_prod : (I : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (_ : Proof (CommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (CommMonoid N)) -> (G : Set) -> (g : Elem G) -> (f : (Elem I -> M)) -> (s : Elem (Finset I)) -> Proof (Eqt N (g (Finset__prod I M s (x3 => f x3))) (Finset__prod I N s (x4 => g (f x4)))).

(; //LEAN:map_sum ;)
map_sum : (I : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid N)) -> (G : Set) -> (g : Elem G) -> (f : (Elem I -> M)) -> (s : Elem (Finset I)) -> Proof (Eqt N (g (Finset__sum I M s (x3 => f x3))) (Finset__sum I N s (x4 => g (f x4)))).

(; //LEAN:neg_add_cancel ;)
neg_add_cancel : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (plus (Neg__neg G a) a) 0).

(; //LEAN:neg_zero ;)
neg_zero : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NegZeroClass G)) -> Proof (Eqt G (Neg__neg G 0) 0).

(; doc: Multiplication by a natural `n` on a commutative additive monoid, considered as a morphism of additive monoids. ;)
(; //LEAN:nsmulAddMonoidHom ;)
nsmulAddMonoidHom : (X : Set) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Elem (AddMonoidHom X X).

(; //LEAN:one_mul ;)
one_mul : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOneClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (times 1 a) a).

(; doc: The `n`th power map on a commutative monoid for a natural `n`, considered as a morphism of monoids. ;)
(; //LEAN:powMonoidHom ;)
powMonoidHom : (X : Set) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Elem (MonoidHom X X).

(; doc: The [axiom](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.36.0-rc1/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&name=axioms) of **propositional extensionality**. It asserts that if propositions `a` and `b` are logically equivalent (that is, if `a` can be proved from `b` and vice versa), then `a` and `b` are *equal*, meaning `a` can be replaced with `b` in all contexts.  The standard logical connectives provably respect propositional extensionality. However, an axiom is needed for higher order expressions like `P a` where `P : Prop → Prop` is unknown, as well as for equality. Propositional extensionality is intuitionistically valid. ;)
(; //LEAN:propext ;)
propext : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop a b).

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; //LEAN:top_unique ;)
top_unique : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (PartialOrder X)) -> (_ : Proof (OrderTop X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Leq (Top__top X) a)) -> Proof (Eqt X a (Top__top X)).

(; //LEAN:zero_add ;)
zero_add : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddZeroClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (plus 0 a) a).

(; doc: The `n`-th power map (for an integer `n`) on a commutative group, considered as a group homomorphism. ;)
(; //LEAN:zpowGroupHom ;)
zpowGroupHom : (X : Set) -> (n : Elem Int) -> Elem (MonoidHom X X).

(; doc: Multiplication by an integer `n` on a commutative additive group, considered as an additive group homomorphism. ;)
(; //LEAN:zsmulAddGroupHom ;)
zsmulAddGroupHom : (X : Set) -> (n : Elem Int) -> Elem (AddMonoidHom X X).

